From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:31:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: up to eight concurrent streams,
 TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW (MiaAI-Lab recipe patch 0069)

MiaAI-Lab's recipe (github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold @ 1576746, v1.5) serves up to
eight requests at once with patch 0069-glm-eight-streams: a batched verify window's segment tables (and the launch
grids that read them) hold one segment a stream past four, four as before up to four; the multi-stream drafter,
scheduler and startup estimate take five to eight streams; TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW sets the rows of every stream's verify
window together in a round (32 as before, 16 to 64 in steps of 8) and the estimate counts it. Her measured gain on two
Sparks, 4 -> 8 requests at once: prose 103.2 -> 130.8 tok/s, code 126.7 -> 167.0; "PARALLEL=4 gives v1.4's shas".
This ports the patch's end state (Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026 MiaAI-Lab):

- engine.py: PARALLEL_MOST 8; multi_window (TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW) and multi_decode_rows, verbatim but for the
  docstrings; GlmEngine.multi_window, read on every rank and compared with the other --parallel settings; the
  estimate's token selection scores and decode rows follow the window; multi_draft_bytes counts the drafter's block
  passes and context updates past four streams (0 at four or fewer). The AFD attention node reads the window before
  the expert wire opens.
- segments.py: MAX_SEGS 8, SEGS_DEFAULT 4, seg_count; SegRows keeps four segments unless asked for more.
- verify.py: BatchedVerify(segs=) sizes its segment tables by seg_count(the engine's stream slots).
- multi.py: MultiDecoder.window (default the engine's TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW, else MAX_WINDOW) caps a round's rows,
  sizes the batched window and bounds the joint draft allocation.
- cuda/geometry.py: mla_geometry(decode_rows=), default MLA_DECODE_ROWS (64, the literal it replaces).
- dflash2_multi.py: the docstring's example.

Changes from the recipe: TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW defaults to 32 at every --parallel (her config.sh sets 64 past four
requests); the main --parallel start line names the window only when it is not 32, at its end, so today's line is
unchanged; this tree's 8-row stream windows keep the drafter's 64-row updates and block passes up to eight streams.

With --parallel 4 or less and TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW unset, the segment tables, buffers, startup estimate, settings
compared, replies, cached counts, kept states, pool rows and the forward's kernel calls are the ones before. The
expert nodes and the wire are unchanged: a round's window crosses as one eager exchange of up to its rows.

Tests: tests/test_glm_eight_streams.py (CPU): the settings; the estimate equal to the old formula up to four streams;
segment tables of up to eight; ten staggered requests on eight slots on the AFD attention node (the real
BatchedVerify.forward over the fake model) at windows 32 and 64 and at two ranks, every reply equal to the request
served alone and more than four streams in one window (the window shapes with the TF_GLM_* switches at their
defaults; under the switches a run starts with, the replies and the bounds); eight 8-row streams filling windows of
exactly 32, 48 and 64
rows; GlmScheduler with eight slots under a ten-request burst; a window smaller than its streams refused; AfdEngine
taking 5 to 8 and refusing 9 and a bad TF_GLM_MULTI_WINDOW before the wire. tests/test_glm_multi_afd.py: past 8 is
refused (was past 4). docs/recipes/glm-5.3-flash-afd.md: --parallel 2 to 8.

Co-authored-by: MiaAI-Lab <MiaAI-Lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

